/**
@page usage User guide

This page walks through the library task by task. Every snippet assumes

@code{.cpp}
#include <ctr/CTR.hpp>
using namespace ctr;
using Vec3 = blaze::StaticVector<double, 3UL>;
using Vec6 = blaze::StaticVector<double, 6UL>;
@endcode

and SI units throughout (see @ref conventions). A complete program is in the
@ref fk_ik_demo.cpp "fk_ik_demo.cpp" example.

@tableofcontents

@section usage_tubes 1. Describing the tubes

Each tube is a ctr::Tube built from a ctr::TubeParams aggregate. A tube is a
straight *transmission* section of length `ls` (at the actuator), followed
by a *curved* section of length `lc` with constant pre-curvature `u_ast`:

| Field   | Meaning                                    | Unit |
|---------|--------------------------------------------|------|
| `OD`, `ID` | outer / inner diameter                  | m    |
| `E`, `G`   | Young's / shear modulus (Nitinol: ≈ 50–80 GPa, G = E / (2(1+ν))) | Pa |
| `ls`    | straight-section length                    | m    |
| `lc`    | curved-section length                      | m    |
| `u_ast` | pre-curvature in the tube's body frame; `{1/R, 0, 0}` for a planar arc of radius R | 1/m |

@code{.cpp}
constexpr double E = 65e9, nu = 0.32, G = E / (2.0 * (1.0 + nu));
std::array<Tube, NUM_TUBES> tubes = {
    Tube{{.OD = 0.92e-3, .ID = 0.80e-3, .E = E, .G = G,
          .ls = 190e-3, .lc = 60e-3, .u_ast = {1.0 / 0.040, 0.0, 0.0}}},  // innermost
    Tube{{.OD = 1.10e-3, .ID = 0.97e-3, .E = E, .G = G,
          .ls = 120e-3, .lc = 80e-3, .u_ast = {1.0 / 0.100, 0.0, 0.0}}},
    Tube{{.OD = 1.40e-3, .ID = 1.20e-3, .E = E, .G = G,
          .ls = 90e-3,  .lc = 40e-3, .u_ast = {1.0 / 0.140, 0.0, 0.0}}},  // outermost
};
@endcode

The order is always **innermost first**. Invalid geometry or material
(e.g. `OD <= ID`, a non-positive modulus) throws `std::invalid_argument`
from the Tube constructor, so a Tube that exists is always valid. Tubes are
immutable: to change one, copy its Tube::parameters(), edit, rebuild, and
hand it to CTR::setTube.

@section usage_robot 2. Creating a robot

@code{.cpp}
const Vec6 q0 = {-120e-3, -100e-3, -80e-3,  // β₁..β₃ [m], ≤ 0
                 0.0, 0.0, 0.0};            // α₁..α₃ [rad]
CTR robot(tubes, q0, 1e-6);                 // BVP tolerance
@endcode

The joint vector q = [β₁, β₂, β₃, α₁, α₂, α₃] holds each tube's retraction
β (how far its base is pulled back; tube i then protrudes `L_i + β_i`) and
its base rotation α. The tolerance applies to the non-dimensional BVP
residual; `1e-6` is a good default. The constructor does **not** solve the
kinematics — the tip and shapes are empty until the first CTR::actuate.

@section usage_fk 3. Forward kinematics

@code{.cpp}
bvp_type guess{};                           // zero = valid cold start
const FKResult fk = robot.actuate(q0, guess);
if (!fk) {
    // fk.status: MaxIterations or NumericalError; fk.residual: how far off
}
const Vec3 tip = robot.tipPosition();       // [m], base frame
@endcode

CTR::actuate stores the configuration, solves the boundary value problem
(see @ref model_bvp), and records the backbone. It never throws on
non-convergence: the returned ctr::FKResult (convertible to `bool`) says what
happened. Always check it before using the tip.

@subsection usage_warm Warm starting

`guess` is an in/out argument: on return it holds the solved shooting vector
(ctr::bvp_type). Passing it back on the next call — for a nearby
configuration — warm-starts the solver, which then typically converges in
one to three iterations instead of many. Keep one `bvp_type` per robot and
thread it through every call; reset it to zero (`guess = 0.0;`) only after a
large jump in configuration or a failed solve.

@section usage_shapes 4. Reading the shape

After a solve the robot exposes the result in several granularities:

| Call | Returns |
|------|---------|
| CTR::tipPosition()      | tip position [m] |
| CTR::backboneShape()    | backbone polyline, one point per integration step |
| CTR::tubeShapes()       | one polyline per tube (each ends at its own distal end) |
| CTR::distalEnds()       | arc length at which each tube ends [m] |
| CTR::states(), CTR::arcLengthSamples() | full 15-dim ODE state at each sample (indexed by ctr::StateIdx) and its arc length |

@code{.cpp}
const auto shapes = robot.tubeShapes();
for (std::size_t i = 0; i < NUM_TUBES; ++i)
    for (const Vec3 &p : shapes[i])
        csv << i << ',' << p[0] << ',' << p[1] << ',' << p[2] << '\n';
@endcode

@image html Backbone.png "tubeShapes() plotted: tube 1 (blue), 2 (orange), 3 (green)" width=60%

The orientation along the backbone is available from states() via the
quaternion components StateIdx::QUAT_W..QUAT_Z (tube 1's body frame; see
@ref conv_frames). Shapes always describe the most recent actuate()/solveIK().

@section usage_ik 5. Inverse kinematics

CTR::solveIK finds a joint configuration that places the tip at a Cartesian
target:

@code{.cpp}
const Vec3 target = {0.0, 0.03, 0.18};      // [m]
const IKResult ik = robot.solveIK(target, 5e-4, guess);   // 0.5 mm tolerance
if (ik) {
    use(ik.q);                              // robot is now actuated at ik.q
} else {
    // ik.positionError: best distance achieved; ik.lastBVPStatus: last FK status
}
@endcode

Things to know:

- **It starts from the robot's current configuration.** The last
  actuate()/solveIK() (or CTR::setConfiguration) defines the initial point.
  The method is local, so a nearby start matters for far targets.
- **Success means the tip is really there**: ctr::IKResult::converged is
  true only if `‖tip − target‖ ≤ posTol` at exit, never merely because the
  last BVP converged.
- **Results are always feasible and consistent**: the returned q satisfies
  the telescoping limits (@ref conv_limits), and on return the robot is
  actuated at IKResult::q, so tipPosition() and the shapes match it.
- Typical cost: a few milliseconds for a fresh target; about 1 ms per 2 mm
  step when tracking with a warm start.

@subsection usage_tracking Tracking a trajectory

For a stream of nearby targets, reuse the robot and the guess, and bound the
iteration budget for predictable latency (see ctr::IKOptions):

@code{.cpp}
IKOptions opts;
opts.maxIterations = 20;                    // default 200 covers full-stroke moves
for (const Vec3 &waypoint : path) {
    const IKResult ik = robot.solveIK(waypoint, 2e-4, guess, opts);
    if (!ik)
        handleMiss(ik.positionError);       // robot is left at the best feasible q
    sendToActuators(ik.q);
}
@endcode

The per-iteration caps IKOptions::maxBetaStep (2 mm) and
IKOptions::maxAlphaStep (0.35 rad) keep each step inside the region where
the linearization is trustworthy; enlarge them only for robots much larger
than tabletop scale.

@subsection usage_multistart Far targets and multi-start

Because the iteration is local, a target on the far side of the workspace
may end in a local minimum (`!ik` with a finite `positionError`). Since the
result reports the achieved error honestly, a multi-start wrapper is short:

@code{.cpp}
IKResult best;
best.positionError = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
for (const Vec6 &seed : seeds) {            // e.g. a coarse grid over α
    robot.setConfiguration(seed);
    bvp_type g{};
    const IKResult ik = robot.solveIK(target, tol, g);
    if (ik.positionError < best.positionError) { best = ik; guess = g; }
    if (ik) break;
}
std::ignore = robot.actuate(best.q, guess); // leave the robot at the best one
@endcode

@subsection usage_jacobian The kinematic Jacobian

CTR::kinematicJacobian returns the 3×6 matrix dtip/dq at the current,
solved configuration — useful for your own controllers, manipulability
analysis or velocity-level planning:

@code{.cpp}
if (robot.actuate(q, guess)) {
    const Mat<3UL, 6UL> J = robot.kinematicJacobian(guess);
}
@endcode

Columns 0–2 are per metre of β, columns 3–5 per radian of α.

@section usage_loads 6. External loads

A point force and/or moment at the tip, in the global frame, is applied on
the next solve:

@code{.cpp}
robot.setDistalForce(Vec3{0.0, 0.0, -0.05});  // 50 mN pushing down [N]
robot.setDistalMoment(Vec3{0.0, 0.0, 0.0});   // [N·m]
auto fk = robot.actuate(q, guess);
@endcode

Unloaded robots use a cheaper 3-unknown formulation automatically; a
non-zero load switches to the full 5-unknown problem (about 5/3 of the
Jacobian cost). Set both loads back to zero to return to the fast path.

@section usage_tuning 7. Accuracy and speed

- **BVP tolerance** (constructor): the stopping criterion on the
  non-dimensional residual. 1e-6 is plenty for position control; tightening
  it costs a Newton iteration or so.
- **Integration step** (CTR::setIntegrationStep, default 1 mm, accepted
  range 0.01–10 mm, clamped): the model is integrated with fixed-step RK4.
  At 1 mm the tip error is ~3×10⁻¹¹ m, so a *larger* step (e.g. 2–5 mm) is
  a legitimate way to buy speed. The shape polylines have one point per step.
- **Root finder** (ctr::RootFindingMethod): keep the default
  `ModifiedNewtonRaphson`; it already falls back to the other methods on
  difficult cold starts. The alternatives exist for cross-checking.
- **Build**: the `release-native` preset (`-march=native` + LTO) is the
  fastest configuration.

@section usage_errors 8. Error handling at a glance

| Situation | Behaviour |
|-----------|-----------|
| Invalid tube parameters | `std::invalid_argument` from the Tube constructor |
| BVP does not converge   | CTR::actuate returns a false ctr::FKResult (no exception) |
| IK target not reached   | CTR::solveIK returns a false ctr::IKResult with the best error; the robot is left at a feasible configuration |
| NaN/Inf in a guess      | sanitized by solveIK; pass a finite guess to actuate (zero if in doubt) |
| `CTR::setTube(idx, …)` with `idx >= 3` | undefined behaviour (precondition, not checked) |

CTR::actuate accepts any q; it is your responsibility to pass physically
meaningful configurations (see @ref conv_limits). solveIK only ever
produces feasible ones.

@section usage_threads 9. Copies, moves and threads

ctr::CTR is a regular value type: copying yields a fully independent robot
(including a fresh BVP solver), and moves are cheap. A single CTR object
must not be used from two threads at once, because every kinematics call
updates its internal state. For parallel work (e.g. multi-start IK or
workspace sampling), give each thread its own copy:

@code{.cpp}
std::vector<CTR> robots(nThreads, robot);   // one copy per worker
@endcode

@section usage_trouble 10. Troubleshooting

- **FK fails from a cold start.** Warm-start from a nearby solved
  configuration and move towards the target in a few steps, reusing
  `guess`. Very high pre-curvatures combined with long overlapping curved
  sections can make the model genuinely bistable (snapping); no solver can
  help there.
- **IK returns `false` with a small but non-zero error.** The target may be
  unreachable or on the workspace boundary; check `ik.positionError`. For
  far targets, seed from a closer configuration or use multi-start
  (@ref usage_multistart).
- **IK is slow for large moves.** The step caps make the cost scale with
  joint-space distance; this is by design. Raise the caps only if your robot
  is much larger than the defaults assume.
- **Shapes look stale.** Setters only store values — call CTR::actuate.
*/
